"[[Dreamlover (song)|Dreamlover]]" was released as the [[lead single]] from American singer-songwriter [[Mariah Carey]]'s third studio album, [[Music Box (Mariah Carey album)|''Music Box'']] (1993).<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.sonymusic.com/artists/MariahCarey/Ones_singles/Dreamlover.html|title=''#1's'' – Mariah Carey – 'Dreamlover'|publisher=[[Sony Music]]|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20010122044300/http://www.sonymusic.com/artists/MariahCarey/Ones_singles/Dreamlover.html|archive-date=January 22, 2001}}</ref> A [[mid-tempo]] [[pop music|pop]] song,{{Sfnm|Nickson|1998|1p=107|Shapiro|2001|2p=78}} "Dreamlover" was identified by some critics as the singer's first attempt into the [[contemporary R&B]] market.<ref>{{cite web | last=Breihan | first=Tom | title=The Number Ones: Mariah Carey's 'Dreamlover' | website=[[Stereogum]] | date=October 26, 2025 | url=https://stereogum.com/2175299/the-number-ones-mariah-careys-dreamlover/columns/the-number-ones | access-date=April 20, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | title=Songbook: How Mariah Carey Became The Songbird Supreme, From Her Unmistakable Range To Genre-Melding Prowess | website=[[Grammy Awards]] | date=September 17, 2022 | url=https://www.grammy.com/news/mariah-carey-discography-albums-songs-butterfly-anniversary-glitter-all-i-want-for-christmas-is-you-queen-music-box-songbird-supreme-songbook | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251117061134/https://www.grammy.com/news/mariah-carey-discography-albums-songs-butterfly-anniversary-glitter-all-i-want-for-christmas-is-you-queen-music-box-songbird-supreme-songbook | archive-date=November 17, 2025 | url-status=dead | access-date=May 29, 2026}}</ref> By the release of her first [[Christmas music|holiday album]], ''[[Merry Christmas (Mariah Carey album)|Merry Christmas]]'' (1994), Carey had already recorded seven songs for her fifth studio album, titled [[Daydream (Mariah Carey album)|''Daydream'']] (1995). She sought greater creative control over her work and increasingly pursued the R&B and [[hip-hop]] influences she had long admired.{{sfnm|Nickson|1998|1p=142|Shapiro|2001|2pp=90–91}}<ref name="c495">{{cite web | last=Harrington | first=Richard | title=A Real Live Fantasy | url-access=subscription | website=[[The Washington Post]] | date=November 9, 1995 | url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/lifestyle/1995/11/09/a-real-live-fantasy/42047f71-9788-4965-b420-e54216751aac/ | access-date=May 4, 2026}}</ref>

Carey had previously worked with close friend and music producer [[David Cole (music producer)|David Cole]] on two of her studio albums, ''[[Emotions (Mariah Carey album)|Emotions]]'' (1991) and ''Music Box'', as well as on her ''[[MTV Unplugged (Mariah Carey EP)|MTV Unplugged]]'' project (1992). After his death in 1995, Carey began writing a song about how losing someone close can change a person's life and perspective.{{sfn|Nickson|1998|pp=57, 105, and 142}} Carey had developed an "initial idea" for the song with [[Walter Afanasieff]] and had already written the [[chorus (song)|chorus]].{{sfn|Nickson|1998|p=143}} As an admirer of their work, she wanted to collaborate with the American [[vocal harmony]] group [[Boyz II Men]], so she set the song aside, saying she did not want to finish it because it could be "our song if we ever do it together".{{sfn|Nickson|1998|pp=143–144}}

[[File:Boys II Men.jpg|thumb|left|alt=Four men seated behind microphones at a table, likely a music group|[[Boyz II Men]] in 1995, the same year they collaborated with Carey on "One Sweet Day"]]

After meeting with Boyz II Men, Carey learned that group member [[Nathan Morris]] had been developing a similar song for the group, dedicated to Khalil Rountree, their late tour manager. According to Carey, it featured "basically the same lyrics" and fit "over the same chord changes". Using Carey's chorus and concept alongside the [[melody]] created by the group, they wrote and composed the song together. The track was co-produced by both Carey and Afanasieff; the latter expanded on the [[melody]] and incorporated additional [[groove (music)|grooves]] and [[Beat (music)|beats]].<ref name="x844">{{cite web | last=Breihan | first=Tom | title=The Number Ones: Mariah Carey & Boyz II Men's 'One Sweet Day' | website=[[Stereogum]] | date=October 26, 2025 | url=https://stereogum.com/2182519/the-number-ones-mariah-carey-boyz-ii-mens-one-sweet-day/columns/the-number-ones | access-date=July 4, 2026}}</ref>{{sfn|Nickson|1998|pp=143–144}} As work on the song progressed, Carey expanded the chorus with additional lyrics intended to make it resonate with those affected by the [[AIDS epidemic]], which peaked in the mid-1990s. Her sister, Alison Carey, had been diagnosed with HIV in 1988 at the age of 27, which strained their relationship.<ref name="x844"/>{{sfnm|Nickson|1998|1pp=137–138|Shapiro|2001|2pp=93–94}}

